    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "claim": "Momentum is defined as the product of mass and velocity ($p = mv$).",
      "evidence": "Standard physics definition of linear momentum.",
      "weight": 0.15,
      "depends_on": []
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "claim": "The ship with heavy cargo possesses greater momentum than the ship with light cargo.",
      "evidence": "Since $m_{heavy} > m_{light}$ and $v_{heavy} = v_{light}$, it follows that $m_{heavy}v > m_{light}v$.",
      "weight": 0.15,
      "depends_on": [
        1,
        2,
        3
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "claim": "The average stopping force is approximately the same for both ships.",
      "evidence": "The ships are identical, implying similar hydrodynamic drag and braking systems; the primary variable changing is mass, not the force-generating mechanisms.",
      "weight": 0.15,
      "depends_on": []
    },
    {
      "id": 6,
      "claim": "The stopping time is determined by the impulse-momentum theorem: $\\Delta t = \\Delta p / F_{avg}$.",
      "evidence": "Newton's Second Law in terms of momentum ($F = \\Delta p / \\Delta t$) implies that for a given average stopping force, time is proportional to the change in momentum.",
      "weight": 0.15,
      "depends_on": []
    },
    {
      "id": 7,
      "claim": "The ship with greater momentum requires more time to stop.",
      "evidence": "Combining steps 4, 5, and 6: Since $\\Delta p_{heavy} > \\Delta p_{light}$ and $F_{avg}$ is similar, $\\Delta t_{heavy} > \\Delta t_{light}$.",
      "weight": 0.15,
